  {
   "k": "mudfne",
   "en": "New Zealand declared war on Germany on 3 September 1939, following the United Kingdom's declaration. Unlike Australia, New Zealand made its own formal declaration."
  },
  {
   "k": "15ird7s",
   "en": "Despite its small population of around 1.6 million at the time, New Zealand made a significant contribution to the war. Over 200,000 New Zealanders served in the armed forces, with the Royal New Zealand Air Force (RNZAF) playing a major role in the European and Pacific theaters. Many New Zealand airmen served with the Royal Air Force, while others were involved in maritime patrols and escort duties."
  },
  {
   "k": "5aus0a",
   "en": "The Royal New Zealand Navy (RNZN) participated in naval operations from the beginning of the war, including escorting convoys and engaging enemy vessels. New Zealand’s Army was involved in key campaigns in North Africa, Greece, and Italy, with the 2nd New Zealand Division distinguishing itself in battles such as El Alamein and Monte Cassino."
  },
  {
   "k": "1lfce5u",
   "en": "After Japan entered the war in late 1941, New Zealand shifted its focus to the Pacific, where it worked closely with the United States and Australia to combat the Japanese advance. While New Zealand itself was not directly attacked, its forces were engaged in major battles in the Solomon Islands and other Pacific campaigns."
  },
